(a) Any resident of the county may file a written complaint, signed and sworn, with the county alleging a violation of this division.
(b) A complaint shall specify the provisions of this division alleged to have been violated and facts alleged to constitute the violation.
(c) Upon receipt of such a complaint, but in any event not later than three working days after receipt, the county shall acknowledge receipt to the complainant, and forward the complaint simultaneously to the ethics board, the person who is complained against and the county attorney.
(d) During any investigation and during any hearing which is conducted to determine whether a violation of this division has occurred:
(1) The person under investigation or the accused may, at such person's expense, be represented by counsel of his choosing; and
(2) The accused or his representative, if any, shall have an opportunity to:
a. Challenge the sufficiency of any complaint which has been filed against him;
b. Examine all documents and records obtained or prepared by the ethics board in connection with the matter heard;
c. Bring witnesses;
d. Establish all pertinent facts and circumstances;
e. Question or refute testimony or evidence, including the opportunity to confront and cross-examine adverse witnesses; and
f. Exercise, to the extent the ethics board, in its discretion, determines to be just and reasonable, any pretrial discovery procedure usually available in civil actions (to the extent authorized by law).
(e) The following principles shall apply to evidence in connection with hearings conducted by the board:
(1) The ethics board shall not be bound to adhere to statutory rules of evidence, but shall be fundamentally fair in its administration of evidence;
(2) During any hearing conducted by the ethics board to determine whether a violation of this division has occurred, all evidence including certified copies of records which the ethics board considers shall be fully offered and made a part of the record in the proceedings;
(3) The accused or any other person under investigation shall be afforded adequate opportunity to rebut or offer countervailing evidence;
(4) The ethics board shall inform the accused or his counsel of exculpatory evidence in its possession; and
(5) The standard of evidence in hearings conducted under this division shall be clear and convincing evidence admitted at the hearing.
(f) The ethics board, in addition to its other duties and powers, may:
(1) With the consent of the board or its designee, appoint a hearing officer to conduct hearings under this division;
(2) Order testimony to be taken by deposition before any individual who is designated by the ethics board and, in such instances, to compel testimony and the production of evidence to the extent it is otherwise lawfully authorized to do so; and
(3) Require any person to submit in writing such reports and answers to questions relevant to the proceedings conducted under this division as the board may prescribe, such submission to be made within such period and under oath or otherwise as the board may determine.
(g) The person complained against shall have 30 days from the day after the county received the complaint to submit a written response to the complaint prior to the ethics board deciding whether to hold a hearing.
(h) Within 60 days following the filing of the complaint, the ethics board shall review and consider the same, and, if a hearing is to be held, shall set a date certain for the hearing to take place within 30 days, unless the accused petitions for and the ethics board consents to a later date.
(i) As soon as practicable after giving due consideration to a complaint, or, if a hearing was held, after the hearing, the ethics board shall take any action or combination of actions which it deems appropriate and which it is lawfully empowered to take, including, but not limited to, the following:
(1) Dismiss the complaint based on any of the following grounds:
a. The complaint does not allege facts sufficient to constitute a violation of this division;
b. The ethics board has no jurisdiction over the matter;
c. Failure of the complainant to cooperate in the ethics board's review and consideration of the complaint;
d. The complaint is defective in a manner which results in the ethics board being unable to make any sound determination; or
e. The complaint is otherwise frivolous;
(2) Determine that no violation of this division has occurred;
(3) Determine that the complaint alleges facts sufficient to constitute a violation of this division and that the ethics board will conduct a hearing, in which case the ethics board shall promptly send written notice of such determination to the accused and to the party who made the complaint;
(4) Determine that further information must be obtained in order for the ethics board to determine whether the complaint alleges facts sufficient to constitute a violation of this division;
a. Conduct its own investigation with respect to any alleged violation;
b. Request the county attorney to investigate the complaint and report all findings back to the ethics board;
c. Schedule the complaint for further review and consideration at a future time certain, in which case the ethics board shall promptly send written notice of such determination to the accused and to the party who made the complaint; or
d. Refer the complaint to any appropriate authorities for criminal investigation or prosecution;
(5) Refer the complaint, along with the ethics board's findings and conclusions, to the board for disciplinary action or other suitable remedial action following any hearing required under this division; or
(6) In the case of alleged violations of this division involving employees of the county, refer the matter to the county administrator for review and possible disciplinary action pursuant to the county personnel policy; it being understood that the ethics board shall have no jurisdiction over matters relating to county employees, but that such matters shall be addressed solely by the county as set forth in its personnel policy.
(j) After it has made its final determination, the board shall issue its written findings of fact and conclusions, and may issue any additional reports, opinions and recommendations as it deems advisable under the circumstances. All such reports shall be in compliance with all state and county laws, including, without limitation, the Georgia Open Records Act. All such reports shall be reviewed by the county attorney prior to their issuance.
(Code 1987, § 2-36; Ord. of 4-1-2003, § 2-16)

